On 2011-12-13 15.20, Bob Koehler wrote:
> In article<jc58b4$to5$1@Iltempo.Update.UU.SE>, Johnny Billquist<bqt@softjar.se>  writes:
>>
>> While you probably are right in this case, Bob, the VT100s
>> VT52-compatibility mode was not 100% compatible with a real VT52.
>> Also, you need to explicitly set the VT100 in VT52-mode before paying
>> the game, or you won't be impressed.
>
>     You can switch VT100 in/out of VT52 mode, jump/smooth scroll, ...
>     via software.  I have a little DCL script I wrote in about 1982 to
>     do that.  All I had to do to get into VT52 mode was enter the DCL
>     symbol "VT52".

Yes. That is one way to explicitly set the VT100 in VT52 mode. :-)
Doing it programatically, or doing it by going into the SETUP is both 
explicit mode changing.

>     I don't know if there are any low level details in which VT52 mode
>     didn't behave like a real VT52.  We had some real VT52 and lots of
>     software that needed them and never ran into a single compatability
>     program, if you knew where the VT52 keyboard was mapped on the VT100
>     keys.

There are some incompatibilities. The obvious ones are the code that 
switches a VT100 back to VT100 mode. A VT52 will do nothing (or 
something undocumented) on that sequence.
Another is that the VT52 have something called page mode, in which the 
terminal automatically stops outptting data after a page full, sending 
an XOFF to the host, as well. And then you press a key to get another 
page full before it stops again.
The VT100 don't have that. Not even in VT52 mode.

Obviously also, a VT100 in VT52 mode identifies itself differently than 
a VT52.

There are probable one or two more things in there, but those are the 
things I remember offhand.

	Johnny


