With opaque stickers over the window? That is the way they are supposed
to be used for production systems. EPROMs used to be popular for
development systems where they were reprogrammed after a short time.

> May seem unreasonable, but that's reality.  I don't know if there is
> some kind of refresh that occurs when they are used, but I have more
> than enough evidence that if they sit idle it only takes a couple
> years for an EPROM to fade.

Here is what Intel says for the 2764A:

  "Data shows that constant exposure to room level fluorescent lighting
   could erase the EPROM in approximately three years, while it would
   take approximately one week to cause erasure when exposed to direct
   sunlight. If the EPROM is expected to be exposed to these lighting
   conditions for extended periods of time, opaque labels should be
   placed over the window to prevent unintentional erasure."

An underprogrammed device would fail sooner. For the 2764A, the
algorithm does a verify with Vcc at 6.25V, which raises the threshold
on the comparator, to verify that there is sufficient charge.

-- glen


