>And, while I am asking, does anyone have skeleton code for a
>simple disk driver for RT-11 (and Ultrix-11 and BSD 2.11) so
>I can try to throw something together to test it at 9600 baud?
>
When DEC provided a sample (simple) disk device
driver for RT-11, the RK05 (RK.SYS) was used as
a simple example.

In addition, the TU-58 (DD.SYS) tape drive which is
based on a serial line interface is also handled as a disk
drive as far as RT-11 is concerned.

Finally, in Ersatz-11, the demo version always includes
the HD (HD.SYS - Hypothetical Disk) device driver
along with the source code HD.MAC file.

Together, all 3 should provide a good idea of what is
the minimum requirement for an RT-11 device driver.

I can't help with Ultrix or BSD.

If you want really fast disk I/O, then run RT-11 under
Ersatz-11 on a current Intel CPU with SATA disk drives.
Even before the drive (RT-11 limits a disk drive to 32 MB)
is cached by the operating system, making a copy of one
drive to a second drive takes under 1 second under
Windows XP - that is all 32 MB being read into the
emulated PDP-11 memory and written to the emulated
disk drive on the physical disk (which can be a 32 MB
portion of a much larger disk file).  I was using a core 2
duo CPU (E8200) and 500 GB SATA2 hard drives.  For
the test, I selected two "disk drives" which were on different
physical hard drives.  After the 32 GB for EACH RT-11
disk drive was cached in physical memory, it took much
less time since the read portion of the operation was
already in memory and the write portion of the operation
was finished by Windows XP after the memory had been
updated, but before the blocks were written to the disk
file.  In addition, Ersatz-11 shows up the significant
overhead of the MSCP interface.  Benchmarks using
DU devices take considerably longer than benchmarks
which use the HD device - which only Ersatz-11 makes
available - although it would be great if someone also
wrote the code for SIMH as well.

Probably the PDP-11 hardware addicts will ignore
the above I/O transfer rates, but the software addicts
like myself do not really care what the actual hardware
consists of.  In fact, there is a relatively new Qbus
board which replaces the CPU, memory and disk
controller that is now able to support this level of CPU
and disk I/O performance available under Ersatz-11

The newest board is the PDQ-1000 and it is currently
manufactured by the Logical Co. and is the fastest
PDP-11 "hardware" available, although I suspect that
Ersatz-11 running under a 32 bit Windows XP operating
system on the newest Intel CPUs will be even faster in
BOTH CPU and disk I/O speeds when the latest CPUs
and SATA disk drives are used.

Jerome Fine

