But I kept it simple in my text by intention. Yes, buffering will help
some with interrupts, but we're still talking huge numbers of
interrupts when we get to high speeds. Which should be obvious from
what I wrote. <br>
</blockquote>
I remember (probably at least 20 years ago so this is very hazy)<br>
looking at the interrupt code for a DZ11 with 16 channels.&nbsp; The<br>
FIFO buffer was set to interrupt on every character, so you are<br>
correct with regard to an interrupt for the first byte to enter.<br>
<br>
However, if I do remember correctly, before the interrupt<br>
code released control back to the CPU (I think this was a<br>
PDP-11/34A, but I would guess that the same code concept<br>
could be used for any DZ or DH multi-channel serial device<br>
with an input buffer), it was possible to determine if more<br>
bytes were in the input buffer at which point, all the extra<br>
overhead of entering and leaving the interrupt handler could<br>
be avoided.<br>
<br>
The net result was that when a number of channels received<br>
nearly simultaneous input (within a few microseconds), fewer<br>
hardware interrupts were produced since the interrupt handler<br>
code detected the presence of bytes already in the input buffer<br>
(the hardware buffer in the DZ or DH multiplexor) and those<br>
bytes were transferred to their software buffers for the tasks<br>
or jobs which were waiting.<br>
<br>
At 128K bytes per second for each input channel on eight<br>
channels, that would be two bytes, on average, every microsecond.<br>
I can't remember the instruction speed on a PDP-11/73, but<br>
it would have to be at least 10 instructions per microsecond<br>
to handle 1 word on the Qbus, on average, each microsecond<br>
and very little else during a sustained burst.&nbsp; Johnny, I think<br>
that you would best be able to comment since my hardware<br>
background is seriously lacking.<br>
<br>
However, if the hardware speed was to be increased by<br>
a 100 by using a PDQ-1000 from Logical Co., then it<br>
might be very possible to handle a sustained burst without<br>
any difficulty.<br>
<br>
I think there there is even hardware for a compatible DH<br>
board for the PDQ-1000, but then the Qbus speed might<br>
be the limiting factor.&nbsp; Johnny, can you comment on this<br>
aspect as well?<br>
<br>
Jerome Fine<br>
