Bob Eager <news0001@eager.cx> wrote:

(snip, I wrote)
>> 800 bpi tapes are not good for portability.  They are very sensitive to
>> head azimuth.  That is, the exact alignment of the head such that the
>> bits come out at the same time.  (All tracks clock together.)

> Sounds a bit like DECtapes! And the clock track along the edge that got 
> crinkled if the tape wasn't on the hub properly just made it worse...

But NRZI tapes don't have a clock track.  They depend on odd parity,
such that every character has at least one transition in it.
(NRZI changes the magnetization for a one, and doesn't change for 
a zero.  On read, flux changes generate a signal in the read head.

Earlier, 7 track NRZI had both even and odd parity variants.
(OS/360 has options to read/write both ways).  Even parity 
NRZI tapes can't read a character with all bits zero, so they
just avoided that one.  There is a table in the IBM 704 Fortran
manual on the character code used in memory, cards, and tape,
where they carefully avoid 00 on tape.

PE (phase encoding) used for 1600 requires twice the flux change
density (3200 fc/i), but is much more reliable.  

GCR, uses on 6250 tapes, requires somewhat higher flux change
rate than the bit rate, but not double.  It is generally more
reliable than 1600 PE.

-- glen



