On 2011-12-09 09.07, David Griffith wrote:
> I've picked up Frotz again and am working on enhancing it and (re)adding
> some more ports: for instance MSDOS.  I then had the crazy idea of
> investigating portability to pdp10 and pdp11 machinery.  Porting to a
> pdp10 would be particularly delicious because that was Infocom's primary
> development platform.  Someone described Infocom's setup as a "fleet of
> red refrigerators".  I'm pretty sure at least the later pdp11s can
> support a Z-machine.  Portability to the pdp8 seems unworkable for a
> variety of reasons.  Lack of a C compiler is one.
>
> So...  Is there anyone out there who I can inspire to port Frotz to a
> pdp10 and/or pdp11?  See https://sourceforge.net/projects/frotz/ for the
> latest git.  The dumb interface should work out of the box, but I'd like
> to get full terminal support going as well.

Eh... You already have ZEMU (ftp://ftp.update,uu.se/pub/pdp11/zemu.tar) 
which implements the Z-machine for both RSX, RSTS/E and RT-11, and does 
most of everything to support V1 through V8.
(The exceptions are that it don't support graphics or sounds.)

All written in MACRO-11. I don't think you could ever port Frotz, but 
you are free to try. :-)

As for getting a Z-machine running on a PDP-8, I'm sure I could do it, 
if I were to restrict myself to V3 games. Just haven't had enough time 
or inspiration to do it.

Sorry, but I don't have any suggestion for PDP-10.

	Johnny
	(Who wrote ZEMU about 10 years ago...)


